It’s late, but not so late the streets are empty. The air is warm and restless, a faint scent of rain lingering like a promise. Lanterns swing in the breeze, their light catching on the slick cobblestones. Somewhere down the block, music spills from an open doorway — low, rough, and threaded with laughter.
You’re standing in the mouth of a narrow side street, half in shadow, watching the main square. People pass in pairs and clusters, their boots clicking, voices rising and falling. The world feels like it’s on the cusp of something — that charged stillness before the first drop of a storm.
And there — leaning against the stone wall opposite the tavern’s entrance — is him.
Not staring at you, not yet. But you know he knows you’re there.